-*-text-*-

Documentation for the language used to define NEWIN3.

The main objects of the machine are registers, busses, adders, PLAs and state-machines.
These are defined by defregister, defbus, defadder, defpla and defmachine.

The purpose of NEWIN3 is to say what objects exist, how wide they are,
and how they are connected.


*) Local names

Every object and language construct can be associated with an
individual state-machine, or it can be global (the name refers to the
same thing for all state-machines in the system).

Every definition of an object or a language construct specifies the
name of the state-machine in which the name is to be accessible; or it
specifies "all", which makes the name global.

The actual code for the state-machines, defined by defpc and
defdispatch, does not specify the name of the machine to which it
applies.  Instead, it applies to the most recently defined machine.
The same is true of DEFCONNECT, which makes random wiring connections.
See DEFMACHINE.


*) Definition of data types

Each object (register, bus, etc) is defined with a data type.  The
data type says how wide the object is.  In addition, a data type can
have subfields.  If so, the subfields of the objects of that data type
are automatically accessible.

A data type is defined by defvector:

    (DEFVECTOR typename machine ...typedefinition...)

typename is the name of the type being defined, and machine specifies
which machines the name is defined in.
The elements meaningful in a typedefinition include

    (WIDTH number)

which specifies the width of (number of bits in) the type being
defined.  The value is evaluated.

    (POSITION number)

which specifies the position of a subfield relative to the containing
data type one level up.  It is not allowed in the top level type
definition.  0 refers to the high order bit, but this is really only a
convention except when dealing with an adder.

    (SAME-TYPE ...type-path)

which says that this field has the same width and subfields as the
other type specified by the type-path.  An example of a type-path is
(same-type foo bar) which refers to the foo subfield of the bar data
type.

    (SUBFIELD name ...typedefinition)

defines a subfield.  It contains another typedefinition, which defines
that subfield.  That typedefinition should specify both the width (or
a same-type) and the position; but if the position is omitted, the
subfield is positioned after the last bit allocated to any other
subfield of this type.

    (DISPATCH-CLASS dispatch-name (VALUES-FROM subfield-names))

says that values which will fit in this field will be assigned to
individual uses of DEFDISPATCH which specify dispatch-name as the type
of dispatch.  In other words, a table is kept of which codes are in
use, and each (DEFDISPATCH ... dispatch-name ...) allocates one.
The VALUES-FROM clause says which subfields of this field may be
restricted in the DEFDISPATCH.

    (VALUES (name1 number1) (name2 number2) ... (name5 first5 last-plus-one5) ...)

is used in a subfield of a dispatch field named in the VALUES-FROM for
that dispatch field.  Its purpose is to allow each defdispatch to
constrain this subfield of the entire dispatch field.  Each name in
the VALUES defines one subclass of the dispatch: namely, the class of
dispatch codes which have, in this subfield, the number corresponding
in the VALUES to the name, or one of the range of numbers.  Each
individual defdispatch can list the names of one or more subclasses,
to require that the dispatch code assigned to it be in all those
subclasses.

If a dispatch-class has more than one subfield named in its
VALUES-FROM, then a given defdispatch may name a subclass from any one
of those subfields' VALUES specs, or more than one, if they do not
overconstrain the dispatch code.  Two overlapping subfields may be
used, but there is no attempt to relate them to each other; each
subclass constrains one subfield or the other.  However, no dispatch
code will be given out more than once this way.

The function (width-of-type ...type-path...) can be used, in specifying
the width or position of a field, to refer to the width of some other
field.  The type-path is not evaluated.


*) Busses

A bus is simply a named vector of electrical nodes.  It will be
connected to two or more other busses.  Each bus has a data type which
controls how many bits it contains and how its bits correspond to
those of other busses it connects to.

A bus can be defined by

    (DEFBUS name data-type machine
      (CONNECTS ...connections...)
      (CONTROL-BITS (bit1 ...connections...)
		    (bit2 ...connections...)
		    ...)
      (STATUS-BITS (bit1 definition1)
		   (bit2 definition2)
		   ...))

The name need not be a symbol; it can be a list instead.
machine specifies which state-machine the bus name is accessible in.
Busses are also implicitly defined as part of the definition of a
register, adder, etc.  Also, use of the VAND, VOR, VNOT, etc. logic
functions implicitly creates output busses for the gates thus defined.

The bus is referred to with the BITVEC construct:

    (BITVEC name)

Inside the definition of a bus, *ME* can be used instead of the name
of the bus.

A subfield of the bus can be referred to as

    (BITVEC subfieldname busname)

If the bus data type has a subfield FOO which has a subfield BAR,
both names must be given in the BITVEC to refer to BAR:

    (BITVEC BAR FOO busname)

The CONNECTS clause is optional and specifies one or more connections.
To be reasonable, this bus or some subfield of it should be part of
these connections.  A connection is simply a list of
bus-specifications.  All those busses are connected together.  All of
the busses connected should be of the same length; if they are more
than one bit long, they should be of the same data type.  For example,
this connection connects *ME* to FOO, rotating one bit around:

    (CONNECTS ((BITVEC ALL-BUT-LOW-BIT *ME*) (BITVEC ALL-BUT-HIGH-BIT FOO))
	      ((BITVEC LOW-BIT *ME*) (BITVEC HIGH-BIT FOO)))

CONTROL-BITS creates conditional connections, routed through pass
transistors, and defines single bit busses, associated with the bus
which the DEFBUS is about, to control those pass transistors.
For example,

    (CONTROL-BITS (FOO connections))

defines a one-bit bus (or "signal") which can be referred to as
(BITVEC FOO bus-name), and says that this signal controls the
connections: they are in effect if the signal is 1.

STATUS-BITS creates "output signals" associated with the bus, and
connects them to the specified definitions, which can be single-bit
buses (such as subfields of the bus being defined) or can be outputs
of combinational logic (see below).


*) Combinational logic

Logic gates are specified by operations which act on entire busses in
parallel to produce entire vectors of outputs.  Each such logical
operation applies to one or more busses, which are its inputs, and
each one effectively defines a new bus, which is the output.  This
implicitly defined bus is used by putting it in a connection.

In other words, the logic specification is contained in a connection,
which connects the output of the logic to other things, and inside the
logic specification are the busses to use as the inputs.

For example, to connect bus FOO to the OR of BAR and the UGH field of
BLETCH, do

    (CONNECTS ((BITVEC FOO) (VOR (BITVEC BAR) (BITVEC UGH BLETCH))))

VOR specifies a bitwise OR.  Similarly, there are VAND, VNOT, VXOR and
VEQV.

One logical operation can be used as the input of another.  This
follows from the fact that the logical expression acts as the name of
the output bus from the gates: it can be used wherever a bus is
expected.

Logic need not always be bitwise in parallel.  It can also be used to
combine the bits of a bus.  The VREDUCE operator, named after APL
reduction, applies an associative logical operation to all the bits of
a bus and produces a one-bit bus as output.  Example:

    (VREDUCE VAND (BITVEC FOO))

specifies the AND of all the bits of FOO.

Some simple arithmetic can be specified just like boolean operations.
The operations VINCREMENT and VDECREMENT output a number one greater
or less than the number on the input bus.


*) Constants

A constant value can be used in a connection or as an input to a
logical operation (essentially, wherever a bus can be used).  The
number represents its binary expansion, in ones and zeros, filled out
with zeros to the size of the other busses that the constant is
connected to or operated on with.  If the number is too large (would
require truncation), it is an error.  A negative number means fill
with ones.

One consequence is that 1 cannot be used to connect all the bits of a
bus to 1.  -1 should be used for that.

A symbol defined with a numeric value (such as a label for an
instruction in a state machine) can be used just like a number.

Example:

    (CONTROL-BITS (USE-REGISTER-A (BITVEC REGISTER-NUMBER) REGISTER-A-NUMBER))


*) Additional connections

To define a connection in which all the busses involved are on an
equal conceptual footing, use DEFCONNECT.  The inside of a DEFCONNECT
is just like the inside of a CONNECTS clause in a DEFBUS, but the
connection is a separate entity in the file, and not regarded as a
part of the definition of any one of the busses involved.

    (DEFCONNECT ((BITVEC FOO) (BITVEC BAR)))


*) Registers

A register is a vector of storage cells, which has an
output bus which contains the outputs of the cells, and an input bus
which contains their inputs.  Defining the register implicitly defines
these two busses.  The registers are assumed to be controlled by a
clock, and may loaded on any clock cycle, but the clock is ignored in
the definition of the register since such details are at a low level
which the description language ignores.  Their outputs are available
"at all times" (though at a low level in the hardware this may not
actually be true).

Registers are defined with DEFREGISTER:

    (DEFREGISTER name data-type machine
      (CONNECTS ...)
      (CONTROL-BITS ...)
      (STATUS-BITS ...)
      (PCLSR))			;optional

Like a bus, a register can have symbol or a list as its name.

The input bus of the register is called (BITVEC INPUT name) and the
output bus is called (BITVEC OUTPUT name).  Multiple-arg BITVEC here
does not have quite the same meaning it has when used to refer to a
subfield of a larger bus.  The two uses of multiple-arg BITVEC can be
combined, as in

    (BITVEC UGH INPUT FOO)

which refers to the UGH subfield of the inputs for register FOO.
There can be no ambiguity even if INPUT is a subfield name, because if
the last thing in the BITVEC is a register name, the thing before it
must specify which bus, and any previous arguments to BITVEC must be
subfields.  If the last thing in the BITVEC is a bus name, then any
other arguments must all be subfields.

In general, the input and output of the register must both be
connected to something if the register is to be of any use.  The
connections to the input will usually be conditional ones.  If so,
then on any cycle on which no connection to the input bus is turned
on, the register contents do not change.  This actually requires
nontrivial hardware, but it is all generated automatically.
